.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NSHD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nshd
.Nd network configuration daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bdv
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the system's configuration for
.Xr nsh 8 ,
which requests it over a socket.
It is privilege separated.
The parent process, as root, binds the sockets and starts the children.
Each child except system runs as a user of its own, so that no child can
signal a child of another role or reach its files:
.Bl -tag -width "frontend"
.It show
Four show processes, each as _nshshow in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
answer the read-only socket.
They answer only with what anyone may see: interfaces and their
addresses, routes and route lookups, the ARP and neighbor tables,
VLANs, bridges, the kernel statistics that need no root, the IPsec
flows, the daemons that run, the version, who is logged in and the
hostname.
They send these as facts, which the client renders.
A long answer delays only the show that makes it, and the show makes it
only as the client reads it.
A show has no pipe to the other children.
It is not
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d ,
because no promise allows the interface ioctls that it uses.
Each opens
.Pa /var/run/utmp
before its
.Xr chroot 2 .
.It frontend
Runs as _nshfront in a
.Xr chroot 2
and answers the read-write socket.
Each client is a session, which sends one request at a time.
frontend passes each client's socket to system when the client
connects.
Before config sees a request, frontend tells system of it if the request
may begin a commit, or if it is an operation, a login, a terminal or a
watch.
frontend also tells system when the request is answered.
A client with _nshweb's uid is the web configurator's session.
Only that session may log in, as a user, with that user's password, and
request the user's terminal.
.It config
Runs as _nshconf in a
.Xr chroot 2 ,
holds the sessions, and requests from system what they need.
A session has the permissions of its user's login class (see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) .
config requests them from system at the session's hello, for the uid
that frontend reports.
config refuses what the permissions do not allow: a show without view,
an operation without operate, and configure, a commit, a rollback, a
load or a daemon's rules without configure.
nsh checks exec, the permission to run programs, itself; config only
reports whether the class has it.
.Cm configure
and
.Cm configure exclusive
also need admin, because their candidate is shared by every session in
them.
A session without admin sees no password hash of a
.Cm login user
line.
The web configurator's session has no permissions until it logs in.
It then becomes the session of the user it logged in as, with that
user's uid and the permissions of the user's class.
A session in
.Cm configure private
has a candidate of its own; the sessions in
.Cm configure
share one, and each sees what the others change in it.
config edits a candidate a line at a time, with nsh's own commands, as
they run in
.Cm configure private .
The running configuration, and the kernel state that the commands read,
come from a snapshot that system takes when a session enters configure
or shows its candidate.
.Cm configure exclusive
is
.Cm configure
while no other session changes the shared candidate or commits any
candidate.
It is refused while another session has it, or while another user has
changes to the shared candidate that are not committed.
Leaving it, or closing its session, discards the changes it did not
commit.
While a commit of the shared candidate runs, no other session changes
it.
Commits run one at a time, across all the sessions.
config plans a commit from a snapshot and checks it.
Then config has system test the daemons' rules that the commit changes,
record the commit, and run its script.
If a snapshot taken afterwards matches the candidate, system saves the
running configuration; if not, system runs a script that rolls the
changes back.
.Cm commit check
stops after the tests.
.Cm commit confirmed
runs its script even if the candidate is the running configuration.
If a snapshot taken afterwards matches the candidate, the running
configuration stays in place, not saved, until the deadline; a commit
that saves confirms it.
A commit outside configure only has system save the running
configuration.
In
.Cm configure terminal ,
the session's candidate is made from the running configuration when
each line arrives.
Each line that changes the candidate is committed, but not saved;
commit there saves the running configuration.
.Ic nsh Fl c
sends its configuration a part at a time.
config makes it the session's own candidate, in place of the running
configuration, and commits all of it or none of it.
A session in configure, but not in
.Cm configure terminal ,
may set a daemon's rules for a routing table in its candidate, as the
daemon's
.Cm edit
sets them.
It sends the rules a part at a time, up to 1 MB in all, and it may also
request them.
.Cm rollback Ar N
loads configuration
.Ar N ,
which system reads, into the candidate; rollback 0 loads the running
configuration.
A commit runs to its end even if its session ends.
A session with view may watch commits, and interfaces and routes.
config then sends it unrequested notices between its replies to the
session's requests.
The notices report a commit saved, an unconfirmed commit with its
minutes, a line of
.Cm configure terminal
committed, and
.Cm configure exclusive
entered or left, each with the user's uid.
They also report an unconfirmed commit rolled back at its deadline, as
system tells config.
They report that interfaces or addresses changed, and which routing
tables' routes changed, from a
.Xr route 4
socket that the parent opens for config, once a second at most.
A session may also watch logs.
system follows each log, and config sends the session the text that
system reads from it as notices, until the session's watch drops the
log.
A client that completes lines itself, as Tab does in
.Xr nsh 8 ,
may request nsh's command tables and the names of interfaces, groups,
bridges and routing tables, if its session has view.
The names come from config's last snapshot; if there is none yet,
config takes one first.
config is
.Xr pledge 2 Ns d
to stdio, recvfd and sendfd.
It reads no kernel state, opens no file and runs no program of its own,
and refuses a line that would.
.It system
Runs as root, and reads the configuration from the kernel, the
daemons and the saved configuration.
Whoever started
.Nm ,
system has only the environment that
.Xr rc 8
gives,
.Ev PATH
.Pa /sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
and
.Ev HOME
.Pa / ,
with umask 022 and working directory
.Pa / ,
and the programs it runs have the same.
Only system writes the saved configuration, which is in
.Pa /var/db/nsh ,
not in
.Xr nsh 8 Ns 's
own
.Pa /etc/nshrc ;
nsh's
.Cm show startup-config
requests it.
It answers config and no one else.
It begins a commit only for a request that frontend reported to it and
that is not answered, or was not answered when its session ended.
system reads the uid it records, and whether a session is the web
configurator's, itself: it calls
.Xr getpeereid 2
on the socket of the session's client.
It accepts a request only while that client is connected.
So a compromised frontend cannot choose who a client is.
It keeps a client's socket only while enough descriptors stay free for
its own work: commits and the streams it runs.
When it cannot keep a socket, it refuses that client's requests.
system checks the permissions of that uid's login class itself, as
config does: an operation needs operate, and a commit, its test and a
save need configure.
It also checks a login by the web configurator itself.
The user must be named by a
.Cm login user
line and must not have uid 0.
.Xr auth_usercheck 3
checks the password, and
.Xr auth_approval 3
checks the account.
The session then acts as that user until it ends, and its commits
record that user's uid.
system starts such a session's terminal as
.Xr login 1
starts a shell: it opens a pty, and runs
.Xr nsh 8
on it as the user, with the user's groups and
.Xr login.conf 5
settings, in the user's home directory and a clean environment.
system passes the pty to the session through config and frontend, and
nsh exits when the session closes it.
system runs an operation a session requests, such as
.Cm flush routes
or a daemon's action, if nsh's code accepts it as an operation.
It runs the operation as root, in a child of its own, and streams its
output as it streams a show's.
A child of system also prints what a daemon's control program prints
for a show, and the DHCP leases.
system streams that output only as fast as the client reads it; the
child, and its process group, are killed if the client disconnects
first.
The program is the one nsh's tables name for the show, never one a
client names.
The child runs it as _nshcheck, unless system lists it as a program that
keeps root because it uses a daemon's control socket,
.Pa /dev/pf
or PF_KEY, which are root's.
The child that prints the leases opens them, then becomes _nshcheck.
A log a session watches is followed the same way.
The child opens
.Pa /var/log/daemon ,
.Pa /var/log/messages
or
.Pa /var/log/authlog
as root, then becomes _nshcheck.
It prints the log's last lines, then each line as it arrives.
When
.Xr newsyslog 8
rotates the log, a new child follows the new file.
pf's log comes from
.Xr tcpdump 8
on
.Xr pflog 4 ;
tcpdump keeps root to open it, and gives up root itself.
system checks that the session's class has view, and admin for
authlog, as it checks an operation.
A commit's script, its save, and each test of a daemon's rules run in a
child of system.
If the child runs past its time limit, it fails: its process group gets
SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 10 seconds later, or 5 seconds later for a test.
The limit is 900 seconds for a script, 300 for a save and 120 for a
test.
A script and a save run as root.
config makes a commit's script into edits, and system runs the edits and
never the script's text.
Each edit names one of the commands a configuration has, by its place
among them, with the line's words.
The command is a statement, an interface's or a bridge's line, the end
of a block, a daemon's rules, the state a daemon is left in, or what
follows its rules.
A line of any other command, such as
.Ic reboot ,
.Ic ping
or
.Ic \&! ,
or of a daemon's operation, such as smtp's
.Cm remove ,
has no edit.
config refuses a commit with such a line and reports each one; the boot
leaves each one out and reports it.
system checks every edit before it runs any, and runs none if an edit
names no such command, or names one where it does not belong.
A commit by a session without admin may not change what needs admin
(see
.Cm login class
in
.Xr nsh 8 ) ,
unless the same line was running before the commit.
The script fails at such a change, and the commit is rolled back.
For such a session, a test of a daemon's rules refuses the same lines,
judged against the running configuration.
A test runs as _nshcheck, with its rules in a directory of their own;
_nshcheck owns both.
The tests of relayd, ldapd, npppd, sasyncd and sshd are the exceptions:
they keep root, because these daemons read keys or secrets that their
rules name.
While a test, a script or a save runs, other requests wait, but streams
go on.
system keeps a record of a commit that has begun.
The commit removes the record when it saves or is rolled back.
A commit that does neither leaves the record, and the next commit
reports what it left.
While the record says a commit's changes were not saved, a commit is
refused unless it only saves the running configuration or restores
the configuration from before that commit.
An unconfirmed commit also writes a record of its own.
Its rollback configuration is what ran before the first unconfirmed
commit.
If the commit is rolled back, that record is restored to how it was
before the commit.
A commit that saves removes that record after the save succeeds.
The deadline is the commit's minutes, counted from when the commit
ends.
After the deadline, system rolls the commit back, once no commit it
began is still open and no script, save or test runs.
It demotes carp by 128, runs a script that rolls the changes back, and
promotes carp again once the running configuration matches the rollback
configuration.
If the rollback fails, system tries again 5 seconds later, up to three
attempts in all.
Then the record says the rollback failed, and carp stays demoted
until a commit saves.
When
.Nm
starts, system finds any unconfirmed commit that an earlier run left,
and rolls it back once its deadline has passed.
A commit is refused if the running configuration is not the one it was
planned from.
system runs in a session of its own: when
.Nm
ends, the parent kills every process left in that session, and every
process of _nshcheck's.
.It archive
Runs as _nsharc in a
.Xr chroot 2 .
It stores the shared candidate, and the users who changed it, for
config, in
.Pa /var/db/nsh/archive ,
which it opens before it enters the
.Xr chroot 2 .
config sends it the candidate each time the candidate changes.
config answers the session that changed the candidate once archive has
stored it, whole and synced to disk, or has reported that it did not.
When
.Nm
starts, config loads what archive stored before any session enters
.Cm configure .
.El
.Pp
frontend and config, config and system, config and archive, and
frontend and system each talk on a pipe of their own.
The parent makes the pipes before the children start, so each child
knows who sent what it reads.
Each child also has a pipe to the parent, which tells it when to
start.
The only message the parent accepts from a child is config's message
that the boot is done.
.Pp
If any child exits, or sends the parent or a peer a message that it may
not send,
.Nm
ends: the child's peers find their pipes closed and exit, and the parent
kills the rest with
.Dv SIGKILL .
No child is started again.
If
.Nm
ends while an unconfirmed commit waits, nothing rolls the commit back
until
.Nm
is started again.
.Pp
With
.Fl b ,
as rc starts it,
.Nm
boots.
Before frontend and the shows accept clients, config has system make
the running configuration the saved one, as much of it as takes effect,
as
.Xr nsh 8
.Fl i
does.
What the checks find is reported, and each daemon's rules are tested,
but neither stops the boot.
What config and system print for the boot is logged.
.Nm
returns once the boot is done, with 0 if the running configuration is
then the saved configuration, else 1.
The first time, when there is no saved configuration, system copies
.Pa /etc/nshrc
to the saved configuration.
system keeps a record of the boot, which names the host boot that it is
for.
An
.Nm
started again during the same host boot runs the boot again only if the
boot did not finish or failed.
carp is demoted by 128 until the boot succeeds, or, if it fails, until a
commit saves.
A boot that succeeds removes the record of an unconfirmed commit,
because the running configuration is then its rollback configuration.
If
.Nm
ends during the boot, the boot does not finish, and the next
.Nm
started during the same host boot runs it again.
.Pp
Each child's
.Xr chroot 2
is its user's home directory.
.Nm
does not start unless each user, and _nshcheck, exists, is not root,
and has a uid no other has.
Nor does it start if _nshweb, the web configurator's user, exists and is
root or has another user's uid.
.Pp
Only root and the members of the group nshadmin may connect to the
read-write socket.
Every session there whose login class has view may see the running
configuration.
The read-only socket is for anyone.
.Pp
system does as root what config requests of it.
So a compromised config may request anything that a session may, in a
commit that a session requested: config is trusted as much as the users
of the read-write socket are.
After starting the children, the parent stays root only to stop
system's leftover processes when
.Nm
ends.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Boot, as above.
.It Fl d
Do not daemonize, and log to standard error.
.It Fl v
Produce more verbose output.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/nshd.ro.sockXX" -compact
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.sock
the read-write socket, mode 0660, group nshadmin
.It Pa /var/run/nshd.ro.sock
the read-only socket, mode 0666
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc
the saved configuration, root's
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/nshrc.N
copies of the saved configurations, for rollback; rollback 0 is the
newest
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/commit
the commit record
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/confirm
the record of an unconfirmed commit
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/boot
the record of the boot
.It Pa /var/db/nsh/archive/candidate
the shared candidate, as archive stores it
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nsh 8
